function getValuesFromObjectPath(object, path) {
  let currentObj = object;
  const keys = path.split('.');

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!currentObj[keys[i]]) {
      // Key not found at this level, bail out
      return [];
    }
    currentObj = currentObj[keys[i]];
  }

  const lastKey = keys[keys.length - 1];
  const values = Object.values(currentObj[lastKey]);

  return values;
}
```

The `getValuesFromObjectPath()` function takes an object and a path as arguments, where the path is provided in dot notation. It recursively traverses the object using the keys in the path until it reaches the last key. If any key along the way isn't found, the function returns an empty array immediately. Once it reaches the end of the path, it extracts the values associated with the last key and returns them. Note that this function assumes that the nested keys will be objects containing multiple values, which is why we use `Object.values()` to extract them. If you need to handle different cases, you may want to modify the function accordingly.

